/* The mono stack was written out twice, in the editor body and in the prose
     code blocks. It is a token now because CodeMirror needs it a third time,
     and a theme that restated the stack would be a fourth copy to drift. */

/* Prose and UI body. 420 rather than 400: on limestone, Inter's 400 goes
     slightly thin under greyscale antialiasing. The standfirst is this level
     at 1.25rem, a size alias rather than a ninth level. */

/* Public section headings and archive titles. THE SERIF FLOOR: 2rem fixed,
     never fluid, because "headings 32px and up" has to stay true at 375 as
     well as at 1280, and the face is not cut for less. */

/* Accent: terracotta. Decorative only. It never signals a state. ONE TOKEN
     NOW: a lifted variant and a tint sat beside it, carried contrast pairs of
     their own, and were never painted. Deleted 2026-09-14. */

/* PAPER, GLASS, LIGHT. Ruling 65, Part A. The Hill Country roles above
     stay until builds 2 to 4 migrate their consumers; these are the
     names the redesign uses and nothing reads them yet. */

/* THE FIGURE PALETTE. Four hues, five tints, per theme. Figures only:
     no semantic colour ever appears in one, because a chart series is
     not a state. */

/* The rust, lifted for dark. Chosen to MIRROR the light ratios rather than
       to look similar: 7.59 / 6.80 / 5.94 against 7.62 / 6.87 / 6.34, so the
       control reads with the same weight in both modes. */

/* Mirrors the media-query dark block above. Both are required: one serves the
     explicit toggle, the other the OS preference, and check:contrast measures
     all three declarations. */

/* A percent position along the inline axis. Light sits near the leading
     edge, where the eye enters the control, because the bar is dark and the
     catch is high-contrast against it. */

/* Status dots carried their state in a background, which is exactly what
     this mode removes. The shape survives, and the word beside them was always
     the real channel. A forced border keeps them visible at all. */

/* The affiliation sits under the role as a quieter line: it answers "where do
   they work", which is a fact a reader wants available rather than announced,
   and giving it the role's weight would make the hero two headlines. */

/* No `min-height: 100dvh`. The hero it replaces filled the viewport and put
     everything that answers "why believe you" below the fold, which is the one
     place the proof cannot do its job. */

/* The chrome is a fill, so a focus ring on it lands on brand purple, not on the
   page canvas the default --focus-ring was measured against. Descendant
   selectors are (0,2,0) and outrank the bare `:focus-visible` at (0,1,0). */

/* The error boundary's DEV-ONLY stack. Deliberately not `.prose pre`: this is
   diagnostic output, not writing, and `overflow-x: auto` is what stops a long
   frame widening the document the way the header used to. */

/* See the block on `.site-header` above for the measurement. Wrapping here is
     the half that lets the four labels and three controls break; wrapping there
     is the half that gives them a line of their own to break onto. */

/* THE DEFAULT LAYER: no cookie, so the machine decides. Light is what a browser
   reporting no preference at all falls to, which matches the palette's own
   light-first default. */

/* `[data-enhanced]` is carried too, or it would outrank this by one attribute
     selector and print a fence with 2.25rem of space above it for a button that
     this same block sets to display: none. */

/* Command palette -----------------------------------------------------------
   Enhancement only. Every rule here styles markup created by
   app/enhance/palette.ts, so a reader without script never sees any of it. */

/* The dek. Larger than body text and quieter than the title, which is what a
   standfirst is: the same sentence the meta description carries, so it reads as
   a summary rather than as the first paragraph. */

/* The cover. Full column width with the height left to the image, because D1
   stores no intrinsic dimensions; fixing the width is what bounds the shift to
   one axis. */

/* The series reading path. Two targets side by side under the parts list,
   sharing .post-nav-target with the prev/next pair at the foot of the post so
   there is one bordered-target style rather than two. */

/* The ratified scrim pattern at the LIGHTBOX weight, which is a heavier veil
     than the 45 percent the three dialog backdrops take: an image viewer hides
     the page rather than dimming it. */

/* Search -------------------------------------------------------------------
   Layer 0 is server rendered and must be fully usable with scripting off, so
   nothing here depends on a class a script adds. */

/* The citations. Last in the card, so it takes the auto margin that pushes the
   foot down; where both are present the links row sits directly above it and
   its own auto margin collapses to nothing, which is the intended order. */

/* An h3 under the card's h2. Sized down to a label rather than a heading,
   because it introduces the list rather than competing with the project name;
   the element is a heading so the outline is right, the type is not. */

/* Smaller than the site's other page titles on purpose. A paper title is a
     sentence, sometimes a long one, and the longest here runs to 150
     characters; at page-title size that is four lines of display type. */

/* Who cites this paper. A list of sentences, so it reads rather than tabulates:
   each entry is a title, a venue and a year, and the longest here runs to 50
   entries. Numbered because the order is meaningful (newest first). */

/* The plain-language line. Larger than body text and set apart by space rather
   than by a rule or a tint: it is the author speaking plainly, not a pull
   quote. Sits above the abstract for the reader who will not read the abstract. */

/* The ratified scrim pattern. This site and the admin drawer backdrop wrote
     the same value in two notations, `45%` and `0.45`, which is how one role
     ends up with two owners that agree by luck. */

/* Tightened from 0.75rem: at the 48rem measure the six columns overflowed the
     container by 26px, which clipped the fused total, the one number the table
     exists to show. It still scrolls if a title forces it to. */

/* In and Out, inside a demo's result. An h3 under the demo's h2, so the
   document outline stays ordered; sized as a label rather than a heading
   because it separates two panes rather than introducing a new topic. */

/* The snippet verbatim, deliberately NOT highlighted: this is the input, and
   highlighting it would render the demo's subject with the demo's subject.
   Scrolls in its own box so the page body never scrolls sideways. */
